st also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n{{(\\,l,m\\text{ are coplanar and perpendicular lines}\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,l\\cap m\\ne ∅\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $l\\cap m\\ne ∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $l$ and $m$ being coplanar and perpendicular lines\n\n- Statement III is true. $l$ and $m$ being coplanar and perpendicular lines is a **sufficient** condition for $l\\cap m\\ne ∅$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420679 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420698 1069 "q-172298" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420698 1071 "$\\,$ $|x-2|=6$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x>4$\n- $|x|<10$\n- $x=8$" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420698 1072 #uuid "5ef2cacc-c405-55b2-8430-66b58129ad71" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420698 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =-4,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇏\\,(x>4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then $|x-2|=6,$ but $x≯4.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇒\\,(|x|<10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEach solution of $|x-2|=6$ satisfies $|x|<10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇏\\,(x=8)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then $|x-2|=6,$ but $x\\ne 8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420684 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420699 1069 "q-183429" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420699 1071 "$\\,$ $20∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $10∣x$\n- $4∣x$ and $5∣x$\n- $100∣x$" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420699 1072 #uuid "8e1662ac-5299-5cc7-92ca-68567f121fc8" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420699 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }10∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=10$ is divisible by $2$ and by $10$ but not divisible by $20.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $20$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $4$ and $5.$\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇔\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇏\\,(100∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=20$ is divisible by $20$ but not divisible by $100.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420687 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420700 1069 "q-183485" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420700 1071 "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=2$\n- $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R$\n- $f(x)={x}^{2}$" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420700 1072 #uuid "9dd0c0c2-7652-5690-ae78-53d067f574fa" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420700 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int 2x\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nMoreover, the differential equation ${f}^{″}(x)=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\int 2\\,dx \\\\  & =2x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D\\in R$ is another arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇍\\,({f}^{″}(x)=2)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ we have ${f}^{′}(x)=2x+1,$ and so the statement ${f}^{″}(x)=2$  is true but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 2x.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)={x}^{2})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne {x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420677 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420701 1069 "q-216328" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420701 1071 "$\\,$ If $P$ and $Q$ are congruent triangles, then $P$ and $Q$ have the same area.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the implication above?\n\n- $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles is a **necessary** condition for $P$ and $Q$ having the same area\n- $P$ and $Q$ having the same area is a **necessary** condition for $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles\n- $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles is a **sufficient** condition for $P$ and $Q$ having the same area\n- $P$ and $Q$ having the same area is a **sufficient** condition for $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420701 1072 #uuid "bd31a8b1-49f0-5bbe-9bfb-4daa99eb8e90" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420701 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n{{(\\,P\\text{ and }Q\\text{ are congruent triangles}\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,P\\text{ and }Q\\text{ have the same area}\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $P$ and $Q$ having the same area is a **necessary** condition for $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles.\n\n- Statement III is true. $P$ and $Q$ being congruent triangles is a **sufficient** condition for $P$ and $Q$ having the same area.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420688 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420702 1069 "q-172322" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420702 1071 "$\\,$ $a\\ge 10$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $a=10$\n- $a>15$\n- $a>5$" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420702 1072 #uuid "e4cdd56d-10e6-5abf-be98-585e1ce87428" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420702 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a=10)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a=10,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>15)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a>15,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>5)\\,⇏\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $a=7,$ then we have $a≱10.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420683 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420703 1069 "q-216436" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420703 1071 "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0$\n- $x\\le 2$\n- $x\\ge 0$" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420703 1072 #uuid "ed1378b7-d665-5e7d-b9bb-42ac28cb1db4" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420703 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x({x}^{2}+x-6)=6≰0.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,(x\\le 2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEvery solution of ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$ satisfies $x\\le 2.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x\\ge 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x≱0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420676 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420704 1069 "q-216447" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420704 1071 "$\\,$ $18∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $9∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $6∣x$\n- $72∣x$" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420704 1072 #uuid "f653d451-6beb-59b1-affd-6d539e9553ca" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420704 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $18$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $2$ and $9.$\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇔\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇍\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $3$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $18.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇏\\,(72∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=18$ is divisible by $18$ but not divisible by $72.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420686 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420705 1069 "q-183396" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420705 1071 "$\\,$ If $A⊂B,$ then $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the implication above?\n\n- $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B$\n- $A⊂B$ is a **necessary** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$\n- $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A⊂B$\n- $A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420705 1072 #uuid "fb9b0e1b-3e64-5482-b003-6352af7b5cae" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true] [17592186420705 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n{{(\\,A⊂B\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement I is true. $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅$ is a **necessary** condition for $A⊂B.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. $A⊂B$ is a **sufficient** condition for $A\\cap \\overset{―}{B}=∅.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139534415 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0141819d-fce9-5dfe-8363-7f37fca55fde" 17592186420587 "answer-045755e2-d591-54ee-8420-3178cdc956ba" 17592186420588 "answer-06e8bf70-2a60-5941-87e1-f5189ba873f4" 17592186420589 "answer-09e65e4f-7780-5d04-b434-5b982a0cc738" 17592186420590 "answer-0ca9ed22-18ac-5eeb-9f53-e13bcad40e04" 17592186420591 "answer-0fdd5fd1-d207-54b2-b6ad-11c0a56f64d5" 17592186420592 "answer-14e7d224-a6a4-59f8-8fdd-0ff468f330c1" 17592186420593 "answer-16016d71-db47-5c29-a6f2-a697be2b8d66" 17592186420594 "answer-1c495edd-fe1e-5f10-9062-4276befeff1e" 17592186420595 "answer-1e6db338-0cb7-51e2-ac01-95935810d0ee" 17592186420596 "answer-266cebd5-1df8-59d8-9d7f-5e97061db023" 17592186420597 "answer-2a5084f6-69e4-520c-a5e4-b275a8c4693a" 17592186420598 "answer-2b1c1196-eb9d-51a7-8da8-862fff9a154f" 17592186420599 "answer-2ffdc366-69cf-5944-b0cd-2f7732814b49" 17592186420600 "answer-3513eb3f-f65e-5b39-ab1c-2e0b58c67dc8" 17592186420601 "answer-372fbdd1-3e8c-57d3-b2f8-c9c3f7462ad6" 17592186420602 "answer-3ce83eb5-512d-54ec-a688-0337fd49e4a0" 17592186420603 "answer-3ffe5c1b-c4bb-59cc-9a1d-6ddf172d759f" 17592186420604 "answer-41c86782-467c-5503-8573-af98661637c4" 17592186420605 "answer-45df796e-175e-502d-b952-1965fb870644" 17592186420606 "answer-466de1f8-413a-58e0-83b2-57258605ef5d" 17592186420607 "answer-48cc24d8-8ada-5963-8ea2-3d096ef3b9be" 17592186420608 "answer-49f2aae6-e3f9-535f-82d1-b68b36c62b4b" 17592186420609 "answer-4a289173-5b61-589f-ba66-f52456ff017e" 17592186420610 "answer-4cbca457-7b68-56e6-9d5d-1f73524010a6" 17592186420611 "answer-5970156a-402e-53cd-9465-784a6bc663ad" 17592186420612 "answer-5a3107ee-9464-55c3-b2c0-aa35b357416e" 17592186420613 "answer-60328983-7337-550c-99ac-442094612dcf" 17592186420614 "answer-665a3479-12d